Q3) Explain Confusion Matrix?
A confusion matrix is a table used to evaluate the performance of a classification model by comparing actual values with predicted values.
It consists of four components:
True Positive (TP): Positive cases correctly predicted as positive.
True Negative (TN): Negative cases correctly predicted as negative.
False Positive (FP): Negative cases incorrectly predicted as positive.
False Negative (FN): Positive cases incorrectly predicted as negative.
For bank customer churn prediction:
Positive class (1): Customer exited.
Negative class (0): Customer did not exit.

Q4) Define the following: Classification Accuracy, Misclassification Rate, Precision.
1. Classification Accuracy
It measures the percentage of correctly classified observations out of the total observations.
\[ Accuracy=\frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN} \]
2. Misclassification Rate
It measures the percentage of incorrectly classified observations out of the total observations.
\[ Misclassification\ Rate=\frac{FP+FN}{TP+TN+FP+FN} \]
3. Precision
It measures the proportion of correctly predicted positive cases among all predicted positive cases.
\[ Precision=\frac{TP}{TP+FP} \]

Q5) One Example of Confusion Matrix?
Consider an example of a classification model that predicts whether customers will leave a bank.
Actual / Predicted Positive (Exited) Negative (Not Exited);
Positive (Exited) 40 (TP) 10 (FN);
Negative (Not Exited) 5 (FP) 45 (TN)
Here: TP = 40, TN = 45, FP = 5, FN = 10
Classification Accuracy:
\[ =\frac{40+45}{40+45+5+10}\times100 \]
\[ =\boxed{85\%} \]
Misclassification Rate:
\[ =\frac{5+10}{100}\times100 \]
\[ =\boxed{15\%} \]
Precision:
\[ =\frac{40}{40+5}\times100 \]
\[ =\boxed{88.89\%} \]

Additional viva questions and answers
Q1. What is customer churn?
Customer churn refers to customers leaving or discontinuing the services of a company or bank.
Q2. What is a neural network?
A neural network is a machine learning model inspired by the human brain that learns patterns from data.
Q3. What is ANN?
ANN stands for Artificial Neural Network. It consists of interconnected artificial neurons arranged in different layers.
Q4. What is MLP?
MLP stands for Multi-Layer Perceptron. It is a feedforward neural network containing input, hidden, and output layers.
Q5. What are the different layers of a neural network?
Input Layer
Hidden Layer
Output Layer
Q6. What is an activation function?
An activation function determines the output of a neuron and introduces non-linearity into a neural network.
Q7. What is ReLU?
ReLU stands for Rectified Linear Unit. It returns zero for negative inputs and the input itself for positive inputs.
Q8. What is the Adam optimizer?
Adam is an optimization algorithm used to update neural network weights during training.
Q9. What is an epoch?
An epoch is one complete pass of the entire training dataset through a neural network.
Q10. What is overfitting?
Overfitting occurs when a model learns the training data too closely and performs poorly on unseen data.
Q11. What is underfitting?
Underfitting occurs when a model is too simple to learn the patterns present in the dataset.
Q12. Why is StandardScaler used?
StandardScaler transforms numerical features to have a mean of 0 and standard deviation of 1.
Q13. What is One-Hot Encoding?
It converts categorical values into numerical binary columns so that machine learning algorithms can process them.
Q14. Why are CustomerId and Surname removed?
They are identifiers and do not provide useful predictive information for customer churn classification.
Q15. What is oversampling?
Oversampling is a technique used to increase the number of samples in the minority class to balance an imbalanced dataset.
Q16. What is early stopping?
Early stopping stops model training when validation performance stops improving, helping to prevent overfitting.
Q17. What is the target variable in this experiment?
Exited is the target variable. It indicates whether a customer has left the bank or not.
Q18. Which Python library is used to implement the neural network?
Scikit-learn, using its MLPClassifier class.
Q19. What is the difference between classification and regression?
Classification predicts categories or classes, whereas regression predicts continuous numerical values.
Q20. What is the purpose of a confusion matrix?
It helps identify correct and incorrect predictions and evaluate the performance of a classification model.
Q21. Why do we divide the dataset into training and testing sets?
Training data is used to train the model, while testing data evaluates its performance on unseen data.
Q22. Why is ReLU used in the hidden layers?
ReLU introduces non-linearity and helps the neural network learn complex relationships between input features.
Q23. What is an ensemble method?
An ensemble method combines predictions from multiple models to produce a final prediction that may perform better than an individual model.
Q24. How did you improve the neural network model?
I used two neural networks with different hidden-layer sizes and combined their prediction probabilities to improve classification accuracy.
Q25. What is the accuracy achieved in this experiment?
The basic neural network achieved 86.60% accuracy, while the improved neural network ensemble achieved 86.85% accuracy on the test dataset.
Q26. Why can accuracy alone be misleading?
Accuracy may not properly represent model performance when the dataset is imbalanced. Precision, recall, F1 score, and the confusion matrix should also be considered.
